1. Viability‑Theoretic Foundation

Definition 1 (Settlement Dynamics).
Let the state x(t)\in X\subset \mathbb{R}^n capture resource stocks, infrastructure integrity, population health, and knowledge. The admissible control at x is u(t)\in U(x). The discrete‑time dynamics are

x(t+1) = f(x(t), u(t)),

where f:X\times U\to X is assumed upper semicontinuous and X is compact.

Definition 2 (Viability Constraints).
The constraint set D\subset X\times U encodes all hard limits (oxygen partial pressure, radiation dose, water‑loop closure, energy margin, etc.). A trajectory (x(\cdot),u(\cdot)) is viable if (x(t),u(t))\in D for all t.

Definition 3 (Viability Kernel).
The viability kernel is the set of all initial states from which there exists a control sequence keeping the trajectory in D forever:

\mathrm{Viab}(f,D)=\{x_0\in X\mid \exists \{u_t\}_{t\ge0}, \,(x(t),u(t))\in D\ \forall t\}.

Under compactness of X and upper semicontinuity of f, \mathrm{Viab}(f,D) is closed and can be approximated by the discrete Saint‑Pierre algorithm:

K_0 = X,\qquad K_{n+1} = \{x\in K_n\mid \exists u\in U(x),\, f(x,u)\in K_n \text{ and } (x,u)\in D\},

\mathrm{Viab}(f,D) = \bigcap_{n\ge0} K_n.

Definition 4 (Minimum Civilizational Function Set).
Let \mathcal{F} be a set of atomic functions. Each function f\in\mathcal{F} provides a control subspace U_f(x)\subseteq U(x); the total control space is U_{\mathcal{F}}(x)=\prod_{f\in\mathcal{F}}U_f(x). The restricted dynamics f_{\mathcal{F}} uses U_{\mathcal{F}}. A set \mathcal{F} supports viability if \mathrm{Viab}(f_{\mathcal{F}},D)\neq\emptyset. For an environment E and settlement maturity level L, the Minimum Civilizational Function Set is

\mathrm{MCFS}(E,L)=\operatorname*{arg\,min}_{\mathcal{F}\subseteq\mathcal{F}_{\text{univ}}} |\mathcal{F}|
\quad\text{s.t.}\quad \mathrm{Viab}(f_{\mathcal{F}}, D_L)\neq\emptyset,

where D_L are the viability constraints for level L (higher L imposes stricter constraints).

Theorem 1 (Monotonicity).
For increasing maturity, |\mathrm{MCFS}(E,L+1)|\ge |\mathrm{MCFS}(E,L)|.

Proof. The constraint sets are nested: D_{L+1}\subseteq D_L. Any \mathcal{F} viable for L+1 is also viable for L; the feasible collection shrinks, so its minimum cardinality cannot decrease. ∎

