2. Environmental Constraints and Requirement Generation

The environment is described by a finite set of variables V_E (pressure, temperature, radiation, etc.) with discrete domains obtained by sensibly binning continuous measurements. A constraint is a predicate on an assignment of V_E; the set of all such constraints forms a CSP (V_E,X). Arc‑consistency (AC‑3) enforces global consistency in O(|V_E|^2 d^3) time, where d is the maximum domain size. The habitability intervals H_e for each variable are terrestrial reference ranges; whenever a variable falls outside its H_e, a requirement r is generated.

Lemma 1 (Constraint Soundness).
If an environmental parameter lies outside the habitability interval, the corresponding requirement set is non‑empty.

Proof. By construction: the mapping \phi: e\mapsto r triggers a requirement exactly when e\notin H_e. ∎

Requirements are prioritised via a Quality Function Deployment (QFD) matrix: let active constraints be X_{\text{act}} with severity weights \omega_i and a relationship matrix W where W_{ij} denotes the strength with which constraint x_i demands requirement r_j. The priority of r_j is \rho(r_j)=\sum_i \omega_i W_{ij}, yielding a ranked list. The weights \omega_i and W_{ij} are set by domain experts; sensitivity to these subjective choices is assessed later.

