3. Functional Decomposition with Guaranteed Termination

Axiom 1 (Termination of Single Decomposition).
The decomposition of a non‑atomic requirement proceeds by applying a finite set of rules, each decreasing a strict well‑founded “size” measure. Every decomposition pass terminates, producing a finite tree of sub‑functions. The leaves are atomic functions—capabilities executable by a single agent.

Proposition 1.
Under Axiom 1, the set of all atomic functions \mathrm{Closure}(R) obtained by fully decomposing a finite requirement set R is finite.

Proof. Each requirement generates a finite tree; the union of finitely many finite trees is finite. ∎

The decomposition produces a functional DAG G_F=(F,E_F) where an edge (f_i,f_j) indicates that f_j depends on f_i. Strongly connected components (mutual dependency cycles) are collapsed into a single composite function, and the resulting graph is acyclic. For a composite node, its capability vector \underline{\lambda} is the vector of minima across its constituents, and its cost and trust are aggregated conservatively.

Iterative recursion termination.
After initial decomposition, allocation may introduce new coordination functions (supervision, monitoring). We prove that this feedback loop terminates.

Definition 5 (Recursion Rank).
For any function g generated during the iterative process, let \mathrm{rank}(g) be the length of the longest chain of “triggers” from the original requirements to g in the dependency DAG, where a supervision function created for f is considered one step further from the root.

Lemma 2.
The recursion rank is strictly bounded by the maximum depth of the original functional DAG plus a constant \kappa tied to the settlement maturity level L (e.g., \kappa=2 for minimal oversight layers). No new function can have rank exceeding this bound.

Proof. The original decomposition yields a finite depth. When an automated function requires supervision, a new function is created with rank one greater than its parent. By induction, the rank never exceeds the maximal initial depth plus the number of allowed oversight layers; the latter is fixed by design policies (e.g., “every automated function can have at most one human supervisor”). Thus the process terminates. ∎

Hence the iterative loop (decompose → allocate → generate coordination → redecompose) reaches a fixed point F^* in finitely many steps. The set F^* is the universe for profession generation.

