4. Trust‑Based Multi‑Agent Allocation

We consider five agent types: Human (H), Robot (R), AI (A), Hybrid (Hy), and Infrastructure (I). Each agent a is modelled by a belief over its capability vector \lambda^a\in\Lambda=[0,1]^m. The belief is a pair of lower and upper probability bounds; the trust that a can execute function f with required capability \underline{\lambda}_f is

\mathrm{Trust}(a,f) = 1 - u^a(\underline{\lambda}_f),

where u^a is the upper cumulative non‑exceedance probability.

Reward and cost normalisation.
The reward of performing f is R(f)=\rho(f)\cdot \mathrm{crit}(f), with \rho(f) the inherited priority and \mathrm{crit}(f) a nominal criticality (defined later). The cost of assigning f to a is a convex combination of energy, risk, latency, and lack of adaptability, normalised to the interval [0,1]. Both reward and cost are dimensionless and scaled so that their magnitudes are comparable; the allocation utility is

U(a,f) = \mathrm{Trust}(a,f)\cdot R(f) - C(a,f).

Allocation rule.
For each function f,

\alpha^*(f) = \arg\max_{a\in\{\mathrm{H,R,AI,Hy,I}\}} U(a,f).

The allocation problem is a variant of the multi‑robot task allocation (MRTA) problem, known to be NP‑hard in its general form (Korsah, Stentz & Dias, 2013; Gerkey & Matarić, 2004). We therefore employ a greedy auction heuristic that respects the DAG’s topological order: functions are processed in reverse topological order (from sinks upward), ensuring that when a function is assigned, all its dependants have already been allocated. The greedy choice at each step is \alpha^*(f) as above.

Lemma 3.
If for every function in \mathrm{MCFS} there exists at least one agent with \mathrm{Trust}(a,f)>\tau_{\min}, then the greedy allocation yields a viable control sequence with high probability.

Proof. Traversing the DAG in reverse topological order guarantees that the necessary outputs are available. By definition of the viability kernel, the existence of a capable agent for each function ensures a feasible control; the greedy selection maximises a lower bound on success probability. ∎

When an automated agent is selected, the algorithm generates new supervision functions according to Lemma 2’s bounded recursion, guaranteeing termination.

