6. Dependency Graph and Criticality

The selected professions form a DAG G_P=(P,E_P), where an edge P_i\to P_j means P_j depends on outputs of P_i. The criticality of a profession is initially measured by betweenness centrality on this DAG, but we refine it by considering the time and resource demands of its functions.

Definition 7 (Resource‑Unconstrained Critical Path).
Assign each function a nominal duration \tau(f). The critical path length of the functional DAG, assuming unlimited resources, is the maximum sum of durations along any path from a source to a sink. This can be computed in O(|F|+|E_F|) by standard CPM. The criticality of a profession is its contribution to the overall critical path: professions on paths with length close to the maximum are more critical.

Resource‑constrained scheduling.
In a real settlement, crew‑hours, energy, and equipment are limited. The problem of finding the true critical path under resource constraints is the Resource‑Constrained Project Scheduling Problem (RCPSP), which is NP‑hard (Blazewicz et al., 1983). As a tractable heuristic, we use the resource‑unconstrained critical path as a lower bound and augment it with a penalty proportional to the resource load the profession places on the most constrained resource. The final criticality score \mathrm{crit}(f) is a convex combination of the unconstrained path length and a resource bottleneck indicator. For small instances (|F|\le 500), an exact RCPSP solver can be substituted; the pipeline’s modularity allows this replacement without altering the theoretical framework.

Profession dependency resilience.
We extend the graph with function‑level nodes to apply Menger’s theorem.

Theorem 3 (k‑Resilience Lower Bound).
Let the extended graph contain, for each critical function f, r redundant professions that provide it. If the number of internally node‑disjoint paths from the source professions to f is at least k, then any failure of up to k-1 professions leaves f covered. Moreover, if the redundant professions are diversified such that their common‑cause failure probability meets the beta‑factor constraint, the system satisfies the desired reliability target.

Proof. Menger’s theorem (node version) states that the maximum number of internally node‑disjoint paths equals the minimum size of a node cut separating sources from f. If disjoint paths \ge k, no set of fewer than k nodes can disconnect f, so k-1 failures cannot eliminate all paths. The beta‑factor condition ensures that the remaining path(s) are not all simultaneously compromised by a shared failure mode. ∎

