7. Dynamic Evolution of the Profession Ecosystem

Between major discoveries or policy changes, the workforce adjusts through incremental reorganisation. We model the profession ecosystem state P as an element of a metric space (\mathcal{P}, d), where d is a continuous weighted graph‑edit distance: each edit operation (node insertion/deletion, label substitution, edge insertion/deletion) has a non‑negative real cost, and the distance is the minimum total cost to transform one graph into another. This distance takes real values and permits fractional steps.

Assumption 1 (Contraction).
The relaxation operator \Phi_{\text{relax}}:\mathcal{P}\to\mathcal{P}, which models workforce optimisation (merging redundant professions, eliminating obsolete ones, streamlining dependencies), is a contraction with constant \alpha<1:

d(\Phi_{\text{relax}}(P_1), \Phi_{\text{relax}}(P_2)) \le \alpha\, d(P_1,P_2).

Remark. The exact computation of weighted GED is NP‑hard in general, but efficient approximations via graph neural networks or spectral embeddings are sufficient to estimate the contraction constant in simulation. The assumption is empirically testable for settlement‑scale graphs.

Theorem 4 (Conditional Convergence).
Under Assumption 1, the iteration P_{n+1}=\Phi_{\text{relax}}(P_n) converges to a unique fixed point P^* from any initial condition, with geometric rate:

d(P_n, P^*) \le \frac{\alpha^n}{1-\alpha} d(P_0,P_1).

Proof. (\mathcal{P},d) is a complete metric space; the Banach fixed‑point theorem applies. ∎

Discovery events are modelled as a marked point process \{(t_k, d_k)\}. At a jump, the ecosystem is transformed by an operator J that adds new functions and professions according to the environment‑to‑function pipeline. Between jumps, relaxation drives the system toward the current fixed point. The whole process is a piecewise‑deterministic Markov process; the state includes the environment and technology levels, ensuring the Markov property.

