8. Self‑Improving DNA Loop with Safety‑Hard Constraints

The Civilizational DNA consists of all tunable parameters: decomposition rule preferences, allocation weights, AHP pairwise matrices (quantised), thresholds \theta_{\text{auto}}, \theta_{\text{crit}}, redundancy minima, and other algorithmic constants. We encode the DNA as a binary string of fixed length; all real‑valued parameters are discretised to a finite grid, making the search space finite. The mutation operator is a per‑bit flip with probability p_m, and we also include a global reset operator that, with small probability, replaces an individual with a uniformly random valid configuration. This guarantees irreducibility: from any state, the optimum can be reached in a finite number of steps.

Safety‑hard constraints.
The thresholds \theta_{\text{auto}} and \theta_{\text{crit}} for civilisation‑critical functions are not subject to evolutionary optimisation. They are set a priori by safety analysis and remain fixed. Similarly, the minimum redundancy r_{\min} derived from the beta‑factor model is held constant. Only efficiency‑related parameters (e.g., cost weights, decomposition rule preferences) are evolvable. This prevents Goodhart‑type drift where the algorithm sacrifices safety for apparent fitness gains.

Fitness function.
For a DNA configuration \theta, the full CWG pipeline is executed on a standard lunar environment, producing a profession set P_\theta. The fitness is a scalar computed from the ten validation criteria (missing functions, redundancies, coverage, etc.), aggregated by a weighted sum with fixed, non‑evolvable weights obtained by AHP from a panel of experts.

Theorem 5 (Almost‑Sure Convergence).
If an elitist genetic algorithm with irreducible mutation operates on a finite DNA space, the maximum fitness in the population converges to the global optimum with probability 1 as the number of generations tends to infinity.

Proof. The Markov chain on the space of populations has a unique recurrent class containing the global optimum, because elitism retains the best individual and the irreducible mutation ensures that the optimal string can be reached from any population. Standard results (Rudolph, 1994) give almost‑sure convergence. ∎

