Conclusion

We have constructed a rigorous mathematical chain from viability constraints to a generative profession ecosystem, with formal guarantees on termination, approximation, resilience under common‑cause failure, and evolutionary convergence. The framework is computationally tractable and falsifiable against reference profession sets, making it a viable theoretical foundation for planning extraterrestrial settlements.

Open Problems.

Verifying the contraction property of \Phi_{\text{relax}} on realistic graphs, improving the RCPSP heuristic to a tighter approximation, and integrating legal/ethical constraints directly into the viability kernel remain directions for future work.

Appendix A – Formal Definitions and Notation

A.1 Environment and State Space

The extraterrestrial environment is described by a vector

E = (E_{\text{phys}}, E_{\text{geo}}, E_{\text{res}}, E_{\text{haz}}, E_{\text{energy}}, E_{\text{comm}}, E_{\text{trans}}, E_{\text{bio}}, E_{\text{human}}) \in \mathbb{R}^{n_E}.

Each component consists of a set of continuous or discrete parameters (e.g. atmospheric pressure, temperature ranges, radiation fluxes, resource abundances). The set of all admissible environments is \mathcal{E}.

The settlement state at discrete time t is

x(t) = (x_{\text{res}}, x_{\text{infra}}, x_{\text{health}}, x_{\text{know}}) \in X \subseteq \mathbb{R}^{n_X},

where the components denote resource stocks, infrastructure integrity, crew health, and accumulated knowledge. The state space X is assumed compact.

A.2 Viability Kernel and Dynamics

The control u(t) is chosen from a state‑dependent admissible set U(x) \subseteq \mathbb{R}^{n_U}. The dynamics are

x(t+1) = f(x(t), u(t)),

with f: X \times U \to X upper semicontinuous and U compact‑valued.

A viability constraint set D \subset X \times U encodes all hard constraints (e.g. oxygen partial pressure > p_{\min}, radiation dose < d_{\max}, energy margin > 0, water loop closure \ge 0.95). A trajectory is viable if (x(t), u(t)) \in D for all t.

The viability kernel is

\mathrm{Viab}(f,D) = \{x_0 \in X \mid \exists \{u_t\}_{t\ge0} \text{ s.t. } (x(t),u(t))\in D \;\forall t\}.

Under our assumptions, \mathrm{Viab}(f,D) is closed and can be computed (or approximated) by the Saint‑Pierre algorithm:

K_0 = X, \quad K_{n+1} = \{x \in K_n \mid \exists u \in U(x),\, f(x,u) \in K_n \text{ and } (x,u) \in D\}, \quad
\mathrm{Viab}(f,D) = \bigcap_{n\ge 0} K_n.

A.3 Functions and Control Subspaces

An atomic function f is an indivisible capability (e.g. “produce breathable air”, “maintain thermal loop”). A set \mathcal{F} of such functions provides a control subspace

U_{\mathcal{F}}(x) = \prod_{f\in\mathcal{F}} U_f(x) \subseteq U(x),

where U_f(x) is the control range contributed by function f. The restricted dynamics is denoted f_{\mathcal{F}}. A set \mathcal{F} supports viability if \mathrm{Viab}(f_{\mathcal{F}},D) \neq \emptyset.

A.4 Requirement and Constraint Satisfaction

Environmental variables V_E are a finite set, each with a discrete domain \mathrm{dom}(v) obtained by binning continuous measurements. A constraint c is a first‑order predicate on assignments of V_E. The habitability intervals H_v are terrestrial reference ranges. A requirement r is generated whenever a variable v falls outside H_v. The relationship between active constraints (those that fire) and requirements is captured by a QFD matrix W with severity weights \omega_i. The priority of requirement r_j is

\rho(r_j) = \sum_i \omega_i W_{ij}.

A.5 Functional DAG and Decomposition

A functional DAG G_F = (F, E_F) is a directed acyclic graph (after condensing strongly connected components) where nodes are atomic functions and an edge (f_i, f_j) indicates that f_j depends on the output of f_i. For a composite node obtained by condensing a cycle, its capability requirement vector is the component‑wise maximum of the requirements of its constituents.

A.6 Capability and Trust

Each agent type a \in \{\mathrm{H}, \mathrm{R}, \mathrm{AI}, \mathrm{Hy}, \mathrm{I}\} is characterised by a belief model (\ell^a, u^a) on its capability vector \lambda^a \in \Lambda = [0,1]^m. The upper non‑exceedance function u^a(\lambda) = \mathbb{P}(\lambda^a \not\ge \lambda) under the upper probability. Trust that agent a can execute function f with required capability \underline{\lambda}_f is

\mathrm{Trust}(a,f) = 1 - u^a(\underline{\lambda}_f).

A.7 Profession and Dependency

A profession p = (F_p, K_p, \mathrm{Resp}_p) where F_p \subseteq F is a set of functions, K_p is a connected subgraph of the global competency graph, and \mathrm{Resp}_p \in [0,1] is the required responsibility level. The profession dependency graph G_P = (P, E_P) is a DAG with an edge P_i \to P_j if profession P_j requires outputs from P_i.

A.8 Graph‑Edit Distance Metric

For two profession graphs G_1, G_2 with nodes from a finite label alphabet, we define the weighted graph‑edit distance (wGED) as the minimum total cost of a sequence of edit operations that transforms G_1 into G_2. Edit operations: node insertion/deletion (cost c_{\text{node}}>0), node label substitution (cost c_{\text{label}}>0), edge insertion/deletion (cost c_{\text{edge}}>0). The set of all graphs with at most N_{\max} nodes and edges (a bound derived from the maximum possible function count for the settlement) is finite, and wGED makes it a discrete metric space; all Cauchy sequences are eventually constant, hence the space is complete. This ensures the Banach fixed‑point theorem applies in Theorem 4.

Appendix B – Viability and Minimum Civilizational Function Set

B.1 Monotonicity of MCFS (Theorem 1)

Proof. The maturity level L determines constraint set D_L. Higher maturity implies stricter constraints, i.e. D_{L+1} \subseteq D_L. Any function set \mathcal{F} that supports viability for D_{L+1} automatically does so for D_L. Thus the collection of feasible function sets for L+1 is a subset of that for L. The minimum cardinality over a subset cannot be smaller than that over the superset, so |\mathrm{MCFS}(E,L+1)| \ge |\mathrm{MCFS}(E,L)|. ∎

B.2 Discrete Kernel Computation

Under compactness of X and upper semicontinuity of f, the sequence \{K_n\} is non‑increasing and closed, and the intersection equals the viability kernel. For computational purposes, X is discretised; the resulting approximation converges in the Hausdorff sense as the grid is refined (Saint‑Pierre, 1994).

Appendix C – Constraint Satisfaction and Requirement Generation

C.1 Arc‑Consistency and AC‑3

The environmental CSP (V_E, X) is solved by AC‑3. The complexity is O(|V_E|^2 d^3), where d is the maximum domain size. After enforcing arc‑consistency, the remaining domain of each variable represents possible environmental states consistent with all constraints.

C.2 Habitability Mapping and Soundness (Lemma 1)

The mapping \phi assigns a unique requirement identifier whenever a variable v takes a value outside its habitability interval H_v. If the variable domain after AC‑3 contains only non‑habitable values, the requirement is forced. If the domain contains both habitable and non‑habitable values, the requirement is conditional; its priority is weighted by the proportion of non‑habitable values. This ensures the requirement generation is sound and complete relative to the discretisation.

C.3 QFD Priority Computation

Active constraints are those whose predicate evaluates to true for all assignments in the surviving domains. Their severity weights \omega_i are derived from risk assessment (e.g. based on time‑to‑lethality). The relationship matrix W is constructed using standard QFD practice: 0‑1‑3‑9 scale indicating no, weak, medium, strong dependence. The priority \rho(r_j) = \sum_i \omega_i W_{ij} is normalised so that the maximum possible priority is 1.

Appendix D – Functional Decomposition and Termination

D.1 Axiom of Finite Decomposition (Axiom 1)

Each non‑atomic requirement is decomposed by rules that map a functional requirement to a set of sub‑requirements and design parameters. A strict well‑founded measure (e.g. “abstraction level”) decreases at each step. No infinite descending chain exists, so every decomposition terminates in a finite tree of atomic functions.

D.2 Finite Universe (Proposition 1)

Given a finite initial requirement set R, each requirement generates a finite tree. The union of finitely many finite trees is finite, so \mathrm{Closure}(R) = F_{\text{init}} is finite.

D.3 Iterative Loop Termination (Lemma 2)

The recursion starts with F^{(0)} = \mathrm{Closure}(R). At iteration k, after allocation, new supervision functions (monitoring, exception handling) are generated for automated functions. Define the recursion rank of a function as follows: functions derived directly from requirements have rank 0; a supervision function created for a function f has rank \mathrm{rank}(f)+1.

Let L be the settlement maturity level. The allowed maximal oversight layers \kappa is fixed (e.g. \kappa=2, meaning no function can require more than two levels of supervision). Then \mathrm{rank}(f) \le \max(\text{depth of initial tree}) + \kappa.

By construction, no new function can exceed this bound; the process halts when no new functions with rank \le bound are generated. Since the total number of possible functions within that rank bound is finite (each supervision function is drawn from a finite set of generic coordination patterns), termination is guaranteed. The final set F^* is the union of all functions generated.

D.4 Acyclicity of Functional DAG

All strongly connected components (mutual dependency cycles) are collapsed into single composite nodes. The condensation of any directed graph is a DAG. Composite nodes are treated as atomic functions for allocation, with aggregated requirements and conservative trust bounds.

Appendix E – Trust‑Based Allocation

E.1 Trust and Utility Functions

For agent a with upper non‑exceedance u^a, the trust in f is T(a,f) = 1 - u^a(\underline{\lambda}_f) \in [0,1]. The reward of performing f is R(f) = \rho(f) \cdot \mathrm{crit}(f), normalised to [0,1]. The cost C(a,f) \in [0,1] is a convex combination of normalised energy, risk, latency, and lack of adaptability. The utility is U(a,f) = T(a,f) \cdot R(f) - C(a,f).

E.2 Allocation Procedure (Greedy Auction)

Functions are processed in reverse topological order of the functional DAG (sinks first). For each f, we compute U(a,f) for all agent types and select \alpha^*(f) = \arg\max U(a,f). Ties are broken in favour of higher trust. The order ensures that when a function is assigned, its dependants have already been allocated, so the required outputs are available.

E.3 Computational Complexity and Hardness

The global allocation problem belongs to the class of multi‑robot task allocation (MRTA) with precedence constraints (ST‑MR‑TA). The general problem is NP‑hard (Korsah, Stentz & Dias, 2013). Our greedy heuristic has no worst‑case approximation guarantee in general, but for submodular reward functions the sequential greedy algorithm gives a 1/2-approximation (Krause & Golovin, 2004). In the settlement domain, experiments show that greedy allocation achieves near‑optimal performance due to the modularity of function capabilities.

E.4 Supervision Recursion

If \alpha^*(f) \in \{\text{AI}, \text{Robot}, \text{Hybrid}\}, the pipeline generates a new supervision function f_{\text{sup}} with rank incremented by 1. This function is added to F and the DAG is updated with an edge f_{\text{sup}} \to f. The recursion bound of Appendix D.3 guarantees termination.

Appendix F – Profession Generation: Set Cover, Hardness, and Beta‑Factor Resilience

F.1 Formal Problem Statement

Instance: Universe F (size n), collection \mathcal{S} of subsets, critical subset F_{\text{crit}} \subseteq F, coverage requirement vector \mathrm{req}(f)=1 for f\notin F_{\text{crit}} and \mathrm{req}(f)=r_{\min} for f\in F_{\text{crit}}.
Goal: Find minimum‑size subcollection P \subseteq \mathcal{S} such that each f is covered at least \mathrm{req}(f) times.

F.2 NP‑Hardness Proof (Theorem 2)

Reduction from Minimum Set Cover (MSC). Given an MSC instance (U,\mathcal{C}), construct a profession cover instance with F=U, \mathcal{S}=\mathcal{C}, F_{\text{crit}}=\emptyset (all req = 1). A set cover of size k corresponds to a profession cover of size k. MSC is NP‑hard; therefore profession cover is NP‑hard. Adding redundancy constraints does not remove NP‑hardness, so the problem remains NP‑hard.

F.3 Greedy Algorithm for Multi‑Cover

```
Algorithm GreedyMultiCover(F, S, req):
  remaining[f] = req[f] for all f
  P = empty set
  while sum(remaining) > 0:
    select S in S that maximizes sum_{f in S} min(remaining[f], 1)
    P = P union {S}
    for each f in S: remaining[f] = max(0, remaining[f] - 1)
  return P
```

Approximation Ratio: This greedy algorithm achieves an approximation factor of H_{\sum \mathrm{req}(f)} + O(\log r_{\max}) (Berman, DasGupta & Sontag, 2004). For our setting with r_{\min} typically small (2–4), the factor is essentially H_n.

F.4 Beta‑Factor Common‑Cause Failure Model and Redundancy Requirement

In a closed settlement, redundant professions (e.g. primary medic and backup medic) share habitat, power, and life support. To account for common‑cause failures (CCF), we adopt the beta‑factor model. Let p be the failure probability of a single profession during a reference interval. A fraction \beta of failures are due to common‑cause events that affect all redundant units simultaneously. The probability that all r redundant professions fail is

P_{\text{all fail}} = \beta p + (1-\beta) p^r.

Given a tolerable risk \varepsilon (e.g. 10^{-6} per mission), the minimum required redundancy r_{\min} is the smallest integer r such that

\beta p + (1-\beta) p^r \le \varepsilon.

For realistic values (p \approx 0.01, \beta \approx 0.1, \varepsilon = 10^{-6}), we obtain r_{\min}=2 or 3. This r_{\min} is then used as the coverage requirement for critical functions in the profession cover problem. The factor \beta is estimated from historical data of analogous terrestrial systems (NASA, 2016).

F.5 Scoring and Lexicographic Pre‑filter

All sub‑scores x_j(p) are normalised using fixed‑anchor min‑max: for each score component we pre‑compute theoretical minimum and maximum values over all possible candidate professions (e.g., the maximum possible betweenness centrality in a graph with N_{\max} nodes). The normalised score is

x_j^{\text{norm}}(p) = \frac{x_j(p) - x_j^{\min}}{x_j^{\max} - x_j^{\min}}.

The final score G(p) = \sum_{j} w_j x_j^{\text{norm}}(p), with weights w_j from AHP. The lexicographic filter removes any candidate p from human consideration if A(p) > \theta_{\text{auto}}, unless any function in F_p has criticality > \theta_{\text{crit}}. In that case, the candidate is retained but limited to a “supervisory” role (a separate profession type). This ensures human oversight for highly critical automated functions.

Appendix G – Dependency Graph, Criticality, and Resilience

G.1 Betweenness Centrality

The profession dependency graph G_P is a DAG. For unweighted edges, betweenness centrality of a profession p is

C_B(p) = \sum_{s\neq p\neq t} \frac{\sigma_{st}(p)}{\sigma_{st}},

where \sigma_{st} is the number of shortest paths from s to t and \sigma_{st}(p) is the number passing through p. Brandes’ algorithm computes this for all nodes in O(|P|\cdot|E_P|). Because G_P is a DAG, the algorithm can be optimised by processing nodes in topological order.

G.2 Resource‑Unconstrained Critical Path Heuristic

We assign each function a nominal duration \tau(f). The critical path length CPL is the maximum sum of durations along any path from a source to a sink. It is computed in O(|F|+|E_F|) by standard forward/backward pass. The criticality of a function is initially the total float (or directly its contribution to the critical path). A profession’s criticality is the maximum criticality among its functions.

G.3 Resource‑Constrained Penalty

Because crew‑hours, energy, and equipment are limited, the true critical path is longer and may shift. The RCPSP is NP‑hard (Blazewicz et al., 1983). As a heuristic, we compute a resource bottleneck indicator: for each resource type k, let U_k be the total demand of function f divided by available capacity. The penalty is \max_k U_k. The adjusted criticality score is

\mathrm{crit}(f) = \gamma \cdot \mathrm{CPM}(f) + (1-\gamma) \cdot \max_k U_k,

with \gamma \in (0,1). This crude heuristic can be replaced by an exact RCPSP solver for small instances.

G.4 k‑Resilience with Beta‑Factor Diversity (Theorem 3)

Extended graph construction. For each profession p, insert an artificial “supply” node s_p that represents the profession’s output. Critical functions are leaf nodes. Add edges from supply nodes to the functions they provide. The source set is all supply nodes corresponding to professions that are “sources” in the original DAG (no incoming profession edges).

Node‑disjoint paths. By Menger’s theorem (node version), the maximum number of internally node‑disjoint paths from the source set to a function node f equals the minimum size of a node cut separating the sources from f. If this number is at least k, then the failure of any k-1 professions (nodes) cannot disconnect f from all supply paths.

Diversity condition. In addition, to ensure that the remaining path is not compromised by a common‑cause event, we require that the redundant professions providing f be diverse: they should not share the same habitat module, power supply, or other single‑point failure sources. Formally, we partition the set of professions into diversity groups based on shared infrastructure. The redundancy requirement is then imposed not just on the total count r but also across groups: at least two distinct groups must be represented among the r providers. This guarantees that a single CCF affecting one group does not eliminate all providers. The failure probability with diversity groups can be bounded by a generalised beta‑factor model.

Together, the topological k-resilience and the diversity condition guarantee the reliability target of Appendix F.4.

Appendix H – Dynamic Workforce Evolution

H.1 Weighted Graph‑Edit Distance (wGED) Metric

Let G_1=(V_1, E_1, \ell_1) and G_2=(V_2, E_2, \ell_2) be two profession graphs with node labels from a finite alphabet \Sigma. The edit operations and their costs are: node insertion/deletion c_{\text{node}} > 0, node label substitution c_{\text{label}} > 0, edge insertion/deletion c_{\text{edge}} > 0. The distance d(G_1,G_2) is the minimal total cost of a sequence transforming G_1 into G_2. Because the maximum number of nodes is bounded by N_{\max} (derived from the finite function universe), the space of possible profession graphs is finite. With positive edit costs, d is a metric; the finite space is complete because every Cauchy sequence is eventually constant.

H.2 Contraction Assumption (Assumption 1)

The relaxation operator \Phi_{\text{relax}} models incremental workforce reorganisation: merging duplicate professions, removing obsolete ones, re‑wiring dependencies. We assume there exists \alpha<1 such that for any two graphs P_1, P_2,

d(\Phi_{\text{relax}}(P_1), \Phi_{\text{relax}}(P_2)) \le \alpha\, d(P_1, P_2).

H.3 Banach Fixed‑Point Convergence (Theorem 4)

Since the metric space is complete and \Phi_{\text{relax}} is a contraction, the sequence P_{k+1} = \Phi_{\text{relax}}(P_k) converges to a unique fixed point P^* with geometric rate. Moreover, because the space is finite, the contraction property implies that the distance to the fixed point shrinks by a factor \alpha at each step; after a finite number of steps the distance becomes zero (i.e., the process reaches the exact fixed point). The bound d(P_k,P^*) \le \frac{\alpha^k}{1-\alpha} d(P_0,P_1) holds, but the actual convergence occurs in at most \lceil \log_{\alpha} (\varepsilon_0) \rceil steps, where \varepsilon_0 is the minimal non‑zero distance.

H.4 Discovery Jumps

The process is punctuated by jumps at times t_k (e.g., a Poisson process). At a jump, the environment update triggers the E→F pipeline, adding new functions/professions via operator J. Between jumps, \Phi_{\text{relax}} drives the system toward the fixed point of the current function set. The whole process is a piecewise‑deterministic Markov process if the state includes the environment and technology levels.

Appendix I – Self‑Improving DNA and Genetic Algorithm

I.1 DNA Encoding

The DNA vector \theta consists of all tunable parameters: decomposition rule selection flags (binary), cost weights (quantised to 0/1/2/3), AHP pairwise values (quantised 1‑9 scale), and efficiency thresholds (but not safety thresholds \theta_{\text{auto}} or \theta_{\text{crit}}). The total bit length is fixed (e.g. 160 bits for a moderate configuration). The quantisation yields a finite search space.

I.2 Mutation and Irreducibility

The mutation operator flips each bit independently with probability p_m = 1/\text{length}. Additionally, with a small probability p_{\text{reset}}, the entire DNA is replaced by a uniformly random valid configuration. This two‑stage operator guarantees irreducibility: from any state, every other state can be reached in one mutation‑plus‑reset step. The chain on the space of populations (with elitism) is thus irreducible.

I.3 Fitness Evaluation

For a given \theta, the CWG pipeline is run on the reference lunar environment. The output profession set P_\theta is compared against the benchmark using the ten validation criteria (missing functions, coverage gaps, redundancy violations, etc.). Each criterion yields a sub‑score s_j(\theta) \in [0,1], and the total fitness is \mathcal{F}(\theta) = \sum_j w'_j s_j(\theta), where the weights w'_j are fixed via AHP and not subject to evolution.

I.4 Convergence Proof (Theorem 5)

The GA with elitism and irreducible mutation on a finite state space is a homogeneous Markov chain. Let S^* be the set of populations containing at least one global optimum. Because the chain is irreducible and elitism ensures that the best fitness never decreases, from any state there is a positive probability of transitioning to a state in S^* (via mutation generating the optimum and elitism preserving it). Thus S^* is the unique recurrent class. Standard results (Rudolph, 1994) imply that the maximum fitness in the population converges to the global optimum with probability 1 as generations \to \infty.

I.5 Safety‑Hard Constraints

The thresholds \theta_{\text{auto}} and \theta_{\text{crit}} are set a priori by safety analysis (e.g., \theta_{\text{auto}}=0.8, \theta_{\text{crit}}=0.6). They are not part of the evolvable DNA. Similarly, the beta‑factor derived r_{\min} is fixed. This prevents the GA from trading safety for efficiency.

Appendix J – Final Integrated CWG Algorithm (Complete Pseudocode)

```
Algorithm CWG(E, L, T, A_state, p, beta, epsilon, safety_thresholds):
  // 0. Fixed safety parameters
  theta_auto, theta_crit, r_min = ComputeFromBeta(p, beta, epsilon)
  
  // 1. Environment → requirements
  X = AC3(discretize(V_E), habitability_intervals)
  R = GenerateRequirementsWithPriority(X, L)
  
  // 2. Initial functional decomposition
  F = empty set
  for each r in R:
      F = F union DecomposeToAtomic(r)   // guaranteed finite by Axiom 1
  G_F = BuildDAG(F)                     // collapse SCCs, ensure acyclic
  
  // 3. Iterative allocation with bounded recursion
  repeat:
      F_prev = F.copy()
      order = ReverseTopologicalSort(G_F)
      for each f in order:
          compute capability vector lambda_f
          best_agent = argmax_{a} U(a, f)
          assign f to best_agent
          if best_agent in {AI, Robot, Hybrid}:
              f_sup = GenerateSupervisionFunction(f, rank(f)+1)
              if f_sup not in F and rank(f_sup) <= max_rank:
                  F = F union {f_sup}
                  G_F.add_node(f_sup); G_F.add_edge(f_sup, f)
      update G_F (collapse any new SCCs)
  until F == F_prev
  
  // 4. Compute criticality scores
  for each f in F:
      compute CPM_crit(f) and resource_penalty(f)
      crit(f) = gamma * CPM_crit(f) + (1-gamma) * resource_penalty(f)
  F_crit = {f in F: crit(f) > theta_crit}
  
  // 5. Candidate profession generation
  Candidates = ClusterFunctions(F, allocation, competency_graph)
  // Score and filter
  for each candidate p:
      G(p) = sum_j w_j * fixed_anchor_normalize(x_j(p))
      if A(p) > theta_auto and not any(crit(f) > theta_crit for f in F_p):
          mark p as non-human (remove from human candidate list)
      else if A(p) > theta_auto and some crit(f) > theta_crit:
          restrict p to supervisory role
  
  // 6. Redundancy-aware profession cover
  remaining[f] = 1 for f in F \ F_crit, and r_min for f in F_crit
  P = empty set
  while sum(remaining) > 0:
      best_cand = argmax_{S in Candidates} sum_{f in S} min(remaining[f], 1)
      P = P union {best_cand}
      for each f in best_cand: remaining[f] = max(0, remaining[f] - 1)
      // Ensure diversity: if a critical f now has all providers from same diversity group,
      // force add a provider from a different group if available
  // 7. Build profession DAG and verify resilience
  G_P = BuildProfessionDAG(P, G_F)
  for each f in F_crit:
      k = NodeDisjointPaths(G_P extended, sources, f)
      if k < r_min or diversity condition fails:
          AddBackupProfession(f, different group) or adjust cover locally
  
  // 8. Dynamic relaxation (if in simulation mode)
  if dynamic_mode:
      P = ContractiveRelaxation(P, G_P, cost_params)
  
  return P, G_P, F_crit
```

Complexity Summary.
Step 1: O(|V_E|^2 d^3). Step 2: finite decomposition. Step 3: each allocation loop runs in O(|F|\cdot |\mathcal{A}|) with a bounded number of iterations. Step 5: clustering polynomial in |F|. Step 6: greedy set multi‑cover O(|F|^2). Step 7: disjoint paths via max‑flow O(|P|^3) or better. The whole pipeline is tractable for settlement scale.

